  "solution": "### 💡 Intuition & Approach\nThe implementation computes **Derivatives of Common Activation Functions** by following direct mathematical principles, leveraging vectorized operations to ensure both correctness and optimal runtime efficiency.\n\n### ⚡ Python Reference Implementation\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef calculus_activation_derivatives(x: list) -> dict:\n    arr = np.array(x, dtype=np.float64)\n    sig = 1.0 / (1.0 + np.exp(-np.clip(arr, -20.0, 20.0)))\n    s_d = sig * (1.0 - sig)\n    return {'sigmoid_derivative': np.round(s_d, 6).tolist(), 'tanh_derivative': np.round(1.0 - np.tanh(arr)**2, 6).tolist(), 'relu_derivative': (arr > 0.0).astype(np.float64).tolist(), 'swish_derivative': np.round(sig + arr * s_d, 6).tolist()}\n```\n\n### ⏱️ Complexity Analysis\n- **Time Complexity**: $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ where $N$ represents the size of the primary input.\n- **Space Complexity**: $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ auxiliary memory for computing and storing output representations.",
